Denote $ (\alpha,\beta,\gamma) = (x+1, y+1, z+1)$</think>
<answer>

By inverse AM-GM inequality, we have 
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
\alpha\beta&=\left(x+1\right)\left(y+1\right)\ge \left(2\sqrt{x+1}\sqrt{y+1}\right)^2=4\sqrt{\left(y+1\right)\left(x+1\right)} \\
\beta\gamma&=\left(y+1\right)\left(z+1\right)\ge \left(2\sqrt{y+1}\sqrt{z+1}\right)^2=4\sqrt{\left(y+1\right)\left(z+1\right)} \\
\gamma\alpha&=\left(z+1\right)\left(x+1\right)\ge \left(2\sqrt{z+1}\sqrt{x+1}\right)^2=4\sqrt{\left(z+1\right)\left(x+1\right)}
\end{split} \\
\Longrightarrow \max\left\{\alpha\beta, \beta\gamma, \gamma\alpha\right\} \ge 4\sqrt[3]{\alpha\beta\beta\gamma\gamma\alpha} = 4\sqrt[3]{\left(x+1\right)\left(y+1\right)\left(z+1\right)} = 4
\end{align}

On the other hand, we have
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
\alpha\beta\gamma&=\left(x+1\right)\left(y+1\right)\left(z+1\right)\ge \left[2\sqrt[3]{\left(x+1\right)\left(y+1\right)\left(z+1\right)}\right]^3=8 \\
\Longrightarrow \sqrt[3]{\alpha\beta\gamma}=2<4
\end{split}\\
\end{align}
Hence, in this case we have $\alpha\beta\gamma>4$. In the above case, let the ordered solution be $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) = (6,3,1)$ and $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) = (5,3,3)$ are two other ordered solutions with similar arguments. Hence the range $R_f$ of $f$ is
\begin{align}
R_f\in \left[21, \frac{42(\sqrt[3]{2}-1)}{3}\right]
\end{align}

